---
// The Cloud skill's showcase. It draws the card's actual argument, which is an
// order of work, not a comparison of deployment targets: steps 1-5 are five
// written decisions that exist only on paper, they converge into one brief
// (step 6) that is read before a single resource is provisioned, and only then
// does step 7 build it as code and prove it by deploying it.
//
// That convergence is why this is not the left-to-right snake the QA and AI
// diagrams use. Five parallel decisions feeding one gate is the claim; a chain
// would say they happen in sequence and that the build follows automatically,
// which is the opposite of what step 6 is for. EKS and the machine already paid
// for appear as evidence inside steps 3 and 5, the same way QA's bug report is
// evidence inside a step rather than the card's organizing structure.
//
// Box text stays plain-English on purpose — this is the one place a
// non-technical reader has to follow the shape of the argument without AWS
// vocabulary. The named disciplines live one layer down in the card's
// `details[].keywords`, and the technical specifics (OIDC, ElastiCache,
// security groups, Terraform) in `details[].body`.
//
// Text budget at these widths: 286w ≈ 33 label chars / 42 sub chars,
// 340w ≈ 40 / 51. Source facts: src/content/cards/cloud.md,
// docs/context/deployment.md, docs/context/decisions.md.
---

<figure class="card-diagram">
  <div class="card-diagram-scroll">
    <svg
      class="topology"
      viewBox="0 0 780 450"
      role="img"
      aria-labelledby="cloud-pipeline-title cloud-pipeline-desc"
      xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
      fill="none"
    >
      <title id="cloud-pipeline-title">Five decisions settled on paper, combined into one plan, then built as code</title>
      <desc id="cloud-pipeline-desc">
        Five decisions are settled before any infrastructure exists. One: what
        the platform has to deliver, and the monthly ceiling it may not cross.
        Two: which identities exist, what each may touch, and for how long —
        access that expires with the job rather than a saved password. Three:
        the target environments — production, staging and development — each
        with its own networking and its own reason to exist. Four: the limits
        every resource has to obey, enforced by something that acts rather
        than something that only notifies. Five: capacity and cost decided as
        one question, worked against the ceiling from step one. Those five
        combine into step six: a single brief the implementing agent plans the
        build from, and a plan I read before a single resource is provisioned
        — which is what caught an AWS default that quietly changes behaviour
        without ever raising an error. Only then, step seven: all of it
        written as code so it can be destroyed and rebuilt from one source,
        and proven by deploying it, with a check that stops the deploy if the
        last infrastructure apply never ran. From there, it's ready for the
        DevOps card's release pipeline.
      </desc>

      <defs>
        <marker id="cloud-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse">
          <path class="arrow-head" d="M0,0 L10,5 L0,10 z" stroke="none" />
        </marker>
      </defs>

      <g class="hotspot" data-detail="objectives" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-label="Step 1, the requirements before anything is designed — open the explanation">
        <rect class="node" x="15" y="14" width="286" height="56" rx="10" />
        <text class="label" x="29" y="38">1 · The requirements</text>
        <text class="sub" x="29" y="56">what it must deliver, what it may cost</text>
      </g>

      <g class="hotspot" data-detail="foundation" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-label="Step 2, who has access and for how long — open the explanation">
        <rect class="node" x="15" y="82" width="286" height="56" rx="10" />
        <text class="label" x="29" y="106">2 · Who has access</text>
        <text class="sub" x="29" y="124">each identity, and for how long</text>
      </g>

      <g class="hotspot" data-detail="environments" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-label="Step 3, the target environments and what each is for — open the explanation">
        <rect class="node" x="15" y="150" width="286" height="56" rx="10" />
        <text class="label" x="29" y="174">3 · The environments</text>
        <text class="sub" x="29" y="192">production, staging, development</text>
      </g>

      <g class="hotspot" data-detail="guardrails" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-label="Step 4, the limits every resource has to obey — open the explanation">
        <rect class="node" x="15" y="218" width="286" height="56" rx="10" />
        <text class="label" x="29" y="242">4 · The limits</text>
        <text class="sub" x="29" y="260">rules it obeys, not things remembered</text>
      </g>

      <g class="hotspot" data-detail="resources" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-label="Step 5, capacity and cost decided together — open the explanation">
        <rect class="node" x="15" y="286" width="286" height="56" rx="10" />
        <text class="label" x="29" y="310">5 · Capacity and cost</text>
        <text class="sub" x="29" y="328">sized and priced as one question</text>
      </g>

      <!-- five decisions on one bus: step 3's leg is the merge, so the bundle
           leaves as a single arrow rather than five racing into one box -->
      <path class="link" d="M301,42 L340,42" />
      <path class="link" d="M301,110 L340,110" />
      <path class="link" d="M301,246 L340,246" />
      <path class="link" d="M301,314 L340,314" />
      <path class="link" d="M340,42 L340,314" />
      <path class="link" d="M301,178 L400,178" marker-end="url(#cloud-arrow)" />

      <text class="wire accent" x="15" y="362">steps 1–5 are written decisions — nothing is built yet</text>

      <g class="hotspot" data-detail="validation" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-label="Step 6, steps one to five combined into one plan — open the explanation">
        <rect class="node" x="400" y="126" width="340" height="104" rx="10" />
        <text class="label" x="414" y="152">6 · Five decisions, one plan</text>
        <text class="sub" x="414" y="174">combined into one brief</text>
        <text class="sub" x="414" y="192">the agent plans the build from it</text>
        <text class="sub" x="414" y="210">I read it before anything is provisioned</text>
      </g>

      <path class="link" d="M570,230 L570,262" marker-end="url(#cloud-arrow)" />
      <text class="wire accent" x="583" y="250">approval is its own step</text>

      <g class="hotspot" data-detail="automation" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-label="Step 7, built as code then tested by deploying it — open the explanation">
        <rect class="node" x="400" y="262" width="340" height="104" rx="10" />
        <text class="label" x="414" y="288">7 · Built as code, then deployed</text>
        <text class="sub" x="414" y="310">one source, rebuilt from scratch</text>
        <text class="sub" x="414" y="328">the deploy is the test</text>
        <text class="sub" x="414" y="346">it stops if the last fix never ran</text>
      </g>

      <a class="card-jump" href="#card-devops" aria-label="Continue to the DevOps card, where this becomes a deploy pipeline">
        <path class="link" d="M570,366 L570,392" marker-end="url(#cloud-arrow)" />
        <text class="wire" x="582" y="386">ready for DevOps CI/CD</text>
        <text class="wire" x="582" y="400">&amp; application deployment</text>
      </a>

      <text class="wire accent" x="15" y="424">An assistant compared the options, modelled the costs and drafted the Terraform</text>
      <text class="wire" x="15" y="440">accepting or rejecting what came back needs someone holding both the business and the technical requirement</text>
    </svg>
  </div>
  <figcaption class="card-diagram-caption">
    Click any step to read what it means.
  </figcaption>
</figure>
